      "intro": "Similar toy-brick connections can belong to different electrical and control systems. Start with the electronic family, not whether the motor mechanically fits your build.",
      "notes": [
        {
          "heading": "Connector family first, software function second",
          "text": "The official City FAQ distinguishes Power Functions infrared/control wiring from Powered Up Bluetooth and its changed connectors. A direct PF-to-Powered-Up connection is not established by their shared LEGO brand. Conversely, a supported physical-family route does not prove that every app can read a sensor mode or control position."
        },
        {
          "heading": "The NXT / EV3 exception is directional",
          "text": "Official EV3 support lists NXT and EV3 motors as cross-compatible, and standard LEGO NXT sensors as usable with EV3. EV3 sensors are not supported by the NXT brick. Third-party sensors, driver blocks and unusual component revisions still require their exact documentation."
        },
        {
          "heading": "A working motor is not measured position",
          "text": "For motor position or angle feedback, verify that the exact motor has rotation sensing and that the hub/software can expose it. A basic motor-power lead does not manufacture sensor data. Lights and sensors are different component jobs, not alternative names for a motor."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Adapters are not universal generation bridges",
          "text": "A documented adapter route needs direction, required functions, exact endpoints and software support. This tool does not supply a pinout, resistor value, cable modification or power-source conversion. Unknown adapters remain a verification task, even if their plugs look appropriate."
        },
        {
          "heading": "A useful starting example",
          "text": "The default PF motor to Powered Up direct path is a system conflict. NXT motor to EV3 direct identifies a documented motor-family route; changing that component to an EV3 sensor and receiving brick to NXT demonstrates the asymmetric sensor restriction."
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "LEGO City FAQ — Powered Up versus Power Functions",
          "url": "https://www.lego.com/en-au/themes/city/faq"
        },
        {
          "title": "LEGO Education EV3 FAQ — NXT compatibility",
          "url": "https://education.lego.com/en-us/product-resources/mindstorms-ev3/troubleshooting/faqs/"
        },
