      "intro": "A motor can fit the hub while the proposed remote cannot control it. Choose the human-to-hub control path independently from the motor/sensor connection and port worksheet.",
      "notes": [
        {
          "heading": "Known stock routes are deliberately narrow",
          "text": "City Hub 88009 with Remote 88010 has a documented original-software remote route. A Power Functions IR handset needs its matching PF IR receiver and channel setting; the battery box is not the receiver. Technic Hub 88012 documents Bluetooth to CONTROL+ and compatibility with the Powered Up app. Those statements do not prove a universal 88010-to-every-hub remote rule."
        },
        {
          "heading": "An app needs its actual supported device and hub profile",
          "text": "Choose the app appropriate to the declared hardware and check its current official device guidance. A Bluetooth logo or OS number alone does not prove every product/app combination. The selector does not download software, change permissions, scan Bluetooth or pair a device."
        },
        {
          "heading": "A different runtime changes the control path",
          "text": "Pybricks documentation supports Powered Up remotes on its supported hubs, but that is a Pybricks-firmware capability. It is not a statement about original LEGO software. Merely having that firmware installed also does not establish a loaded/running program that responds to the remote. Choose this route only when it is already installed and documented; no firmware installation is prescribed here."
        },
        {
          "heading": "A program on the hub is a separate goal",
          "text": "The SPIKE Large Hub technical description supports autonomous or streaming operation; the exact software and program still matter. A stock PF receiver is not an autonomous program host. Other stock profiles are not assumed to run a stored autonomous program just because they contain a processor or Bluetooth radio."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Current support and legacy hardware differ",
          "text": "LEGO Education ended direct SPIKE portfolio sales on June 30, 2026, while promising SPIKE App support through June 30, 2031. That is not a guarantee for every future OS or third-party runtime. This workbench supports existing connection decisions, not a purchase recommendation or competition-eligibility ruling."
        }
      ],
